(ROOT/'data/source_qa.md').write_text(f'''# Provenance, QA and publication-vintage audit

- Frozen file: `data/copper_statsmodels_0_14_6_frozen.csv`; SHA-256 `{sha}`.
- Package: statsmodels 0.14.6 (`statsmodels.datasets.copper.load_pandas()`), as installed on 2026-10-27. Source attribution: Jeff Gill, *Generalized Linear Models: A Unified Approach*; the package's documentation says original author retains rights.
- The loader omits the original YEAR column and returns TIME=1..25; **year = 1950 + TIME** was reconstructed from the official package description 1951–1975. No calendar years were inferred from a regression.
- All 25 records have nonmissing, positive inputs. First and last consumption values: 3173.0 and 7157.2 thousand metric tonnes. This is a **curated historical teaching dataset**, not an independently verified official first-release series.
- Historical release timestamps for consumption, metal prices, income, and inventory: **UNKNOWN**. Historical revision chronology: **UNKNOWN**. Lagging each predictor one annual observation does not establish point-in-time availability.
- Price units are not fully described; the factor uses **differences in log changes**, so fixed unit scales cancel. No same-unit level ratio assumption enters the model.
- No independent current copper-market figures are joined to 1951–1975 observations. The ICA 2020 substitution fact is contextual, not historical evidence for the tested era.
- Dataset redistribution rights beyond the statsmodels package's express permission are not independently established. Source attribution is preserved; review rights before commercial reuse.
- Firm exposure, corporate fundamentals, equity total returns, transaction costs, and historical tradable universe: **NOT OBSERVED**.
''')
status='REPRODUCED RETROSPECTIVE WORLD COPPER DEMAND PILOT — NOT PIT / NOT EQUITY ALPHA'
sections={
'01. Abstract':f'''**Question:** Does a one-year-stale copper-versus-aluminum relative-price relief factor improve next-year world copper-consumption growth forecasts? In 10 purged expanding-window origins (1965–1974), the factor-augmented model reached **{float(score['full']['rmse']):.3f} log-pp RMSE**, against **{float(score['controls']['rmse']):.3f}** for demand/income/inventory controls and **{float(score['mean']['rmse']):.3f}** for a historical mean. The **{S['rmse_gain_controls_minus_full']:.4f} log-pp** improvement is economically negligible, its block-bootstrap interval crosses zero, and longer horizons fail. **Verdict: historical macro-demand association only; no reliable lead factor or equity alpha.**''',
'02. Research Design & Hypotheses':'''**H1 (substitution):** lower lagged copper-price growth relative to aluminum predicts stronger next-year copper consumption growth. **H2 (incremental):** adding relative-cost relief to lagged consumption, income and inventory beats the fixed control model on purged expanding OOS RMSE. **H3 (stability):** improvement retains its sign across 2–3-year horizons, 1–3-year staleness, 1965–69/1970–74, block shuffles and weight perturbations. **Null:** no incremental predictive gain. Counterthesis: copper price reacts to demand/supply contemporaneously, substitution is technologically constrained, and annual aggregates are too coarse.''',
'03. Industry Context':'''The original dataset describes **world copper consumption, 1951–1975** (3,173.0 to 7,157.2 thousand metric tonnes, *historical series only*), not current market size. Copper and aluminum compete in some industrial uses, while copper conductivity and application constraints limit substitution. The International Copper Association reported low substitution for **2020**, but this is **not** used as evidence for 1951–1975; the International Copper Study Group factbook is context only. Supply shocks, income growth and inventory cycles offer competing mechanisms; no company-level production exposure is measured.''',
'04. Data & Source Integrity':f'''The **25 annual records** are frozen from `statsmodels 0.14.6` (Gill teaching panel). See `data/source_register.csv` for exact URLs, grades, original-release dates (**UNKNOWN**), observation periods, revision policy (**UNKNOWN**), copyright note and access date. Data QA asserts year continuity, positivity, no nulls, TIME-to-year mapping and spot checks. Frozen raw SHA-256: `{sha}`. Grade B curated dataset, not official contemporaneous vintage; no fabricated corporate or securities data.''',
'05. Point-in-Time Availability':'''At formation year **t**, all predictor features use **t−1 and t−2** only. Labels for a training origin j enter the fit only if **j+h ≤ t−1**; this purges overlapping targets and adds a conservative one-year label buffer. All standardization is computed on the training window only. **However historical actual release dates and revisions are UNKNOWN**, so this remains a retrospective fixed-snapshot experiment, not an authentic PIT backtest.''',
'06. Factor Construction':'''Let \(g^P_{x,t}=100\log(P_{x,t-1}/P_{x,t-2})\). The fixed relative-cost relief factor is **\(F_t=-g^P_{Cu,t}+g^P_{Al,t}\)**, with unit weights and no optimized scaling. Positive F means copper inflation was lower than aluminum inflation in the *prior* year. The target is \(y_{t,h}=100\log(C_{t+h}/C_t)\). Controls are lagged consumption growth, lagged real-income-index growth and lagged inventory-index change. Weight perturbations test both factor coefficients in [0.75,1.25].''',
'07. Exposure & Transmission':'''This is a **single world copper-demand series**, not a stock universe: sector exposure is 1 by construction, not an estimated firm loading. Aluminum substitution is a hypothesized transmission channel; income and inventory are confounders. `data/exposure_map.csv` explicitly labels mining equities and firm-level weights as **NOT ESTIMATED**. A leave-one-company-out test is **not applicable**; leave-one-OOS-year-out is supplied instead. No investable exposure is asserted.''',
'08. Outcomes & Baselines':'''Outcome: next-year **world copper consumption log growth (log percentage points)**, with alternative 2- and 3-year cumulative horizons. All models use the **same 10 OOS origins** for the primary horizon. Baselines: expanding historical mean; last observable consumption growth (persistence); fixed Ridge (alpha=8) on lagged consumption, income and inventory; factor-only Ridge; and augmented Ridge with the same controls plus F. Training-only standardization, no tuned hyperparameters or full-sample z-score.''',
'09. Empirical Findings':f'''OOS origins **1965–1974 (n=10)**: historical mean **{float(score['mean']['rmse']):.3f}**, persistence **{float(score['persistence']['rmse']):.3f}**, controls **{float(score['controls']['rmse']):.3f}**, full **{float(score['full']['rmse']):.3f}**, factor-only **{float(score['factor_only']['rmse']):.3f}** RMSE (log-pp). The control-minus-full gain **{S['rmse_gain_controls_minus_full']:.4f} log-pp** is tiny relative to errors. These are **10 historical annual observations**, not a statistically powerful out-of-sample panel. `results/oos_predictions.csv` contains each forecast, training size and final admissible label year.''',
'10. Robustness & Placebos':f'''**1,000** three-year-block reordering placebo refits yielded exploratory upper-tail fraction **{S['block_shuffle_tail_fraction_exploratory']:.3f}** (not an exact p-value). **2,000** paired circular three-year-block bootstrap draws gave a 95% percentile interval **[{S['bootstrap_95pct_percentiles'][0]:.4f}, {S['bootstrap_95pct_percentiles'][1]:.4f}] log-pp**, crossing zero. **1,000** independent copper/aluminum weight perturbations were positive **{100*S['exposure_positive_fraction']:.1f}%** of the time; this is not independent evidence because weights are near the original. Leave-one-year-out, no-income/no-inventory ablations and 2–3-year horizons are computed in `results/`.''',
'11. Regime Sensitivity':f'''1965–1969 (n=5) control-minus-full RMSE gain **{float(reg[0]['rmse_gain']):+.4f}**, 1970–1974 (n=5) **{float(reg[1]['rmse_gain']):+.4f}**. Excluding formation years 1973–1974 yields **{float(reg[2]['rmse_gain']):+.4f}** (n=8). The two-year horizon gain is **{float(rob[1]['rmse_gain']):+.4f}** and three-year gain **{float(rob[2]['rmse_gain']):+.4f}**. These tiny subperiods are descriptive only; none validates a stable effect.''',
'12. Bias Audit & Verdict':'''**Publication/revision leakage:** original vintages unknown, cannot certify PIT. **Selection:** Gill's 25-year teaching sample, historical endpoint and a single world series are ex-post selected. **Endogeneity:** prices can react to demand and inventories. **Overlapping targets:** purged training, but OOS errors serially dependent; block bootstrap has only ten origins. **Multiple testing:** horizon/lag/ablation checks are exploratory and not corrected; do not promote best subgroup. **Survivorship:** no security universe exists. **Costs/capacity:** no tradable strategy or transaction costs. **Verdict:** tiny, unstable retrospective demand forecast gain; neither validated modern copper signal nor equity alpha.''',
'13. Reproduction':'''From this folder: `python run_analysis.py`, `python -m pytest -q tests`, and `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute research.ipynb --output reproduced.ipynb`. All source and output tables are versioned. The code uses deterministic RNG seed **20261027** and fixed Ridge alpha **8.0**. Logs under `logs/` record actual execution. From repository root run `python scripts/validate_studies.py`; its structural success does **not** prove PIT validity.''',
'14. Next-Stage Protocol':'''Obtain monthly first-release refined-copper usage from ICSG, original-date aluminum/copper spot/futures quotes, independently dated GDP/industrial output and inventories; freeze publication timestamps and revision vintages. Build a preregistered modern 2000–2026 monthly design with expanding purged OOS, holdout post-2022, sector-neutral mining/smelting company exposure from contemporaneous filings and delisting-inclusive total returns. Include futures carry, slippage, fees, capacity, multiple-testing adjustment and genuine historical constituent universes. Until then, **do not trade on this pilot**.''',
'15. Sources & Artifacts':'''**Primary:** [statsmodels World Copper Market 1951–1975](https://www.statsmodels.org/0.9.0/datasets/generated/copper.html), Gill dataset; access 2026-10-27, original release date UNKNOWN. **Context:** [ICSG Factbook 2023](https://icsg.org/download/factbook2023/) (published 2023-10-09) and [International Copper Association substitution note](https://internationalcopper.org/resource/copper-substitution-remains-low-in-2020/) (2021-04-13). **Artifacts:** `data/` frozen observations, SHA and register; `results/` OOS and 1000/2000-draw tables; `figures/` ten computed SVGs; `analysis.py`, `make_figures.py`, `tests/`, `research.ipynb`, `logs/`. Historical source release dates and revision histories remain **UNKNOWN**.'''
}
