summary=f'''### [Relative Copper–Aluminum Cost Relief — Historical World Copper Demand](Relative-Copper-Aluminum-Cost-Relief-Global-Copper-Demand/)\n\nUsing the 25-year 1951–1975 statsmodels/Gill world-copper panel, 10 purged expanding-window annual origins tested whether stale relative copper/aluminum price growth predicts next-year copper consumption. The augmented model achieved **{float(score['full']['rmse']):.3f} log-pp** OOS RMSE versus **{float(score['controls']['rmse']):.3f}** for demand/income/inventory controls and **{float(score['mean']['rmse']):.3f}** for the historical mean, an economically tiny **{S['rmse_gain_controls_minus_full']:.4f} log-pp** control-relative improvement. A 1,000-refit exploratory three-year-block shuffle gave tail fraction **{S['block_shuffle_tail_fraction_exploratory']:.3f}**, while a 2,000-draw block-bootstrap 95% interval **[{S['bootstrap_95pct_percentiles'][0]:.4f}, {S['bootstrap_95pct_percentiles'][1]:.4f}] log-pp** crosses zero and 2–3-year horizons fail. **Verdict:** a negative/indeterminate retrospective industrial-metal demand pilot, not historical PIT or investable equity alpha; original release vintages, firm fundamentals and total returns are absent.\n'''
